    18	
    19	He spent the second day writing one letter.
    20	
    21	It went to a man in the western stores whom he had known in a posting twelve years before and had not corresponded with in nine, and it was three sentences about a requisition number that did not exist and a polite query as to whether the number belonged to the Halcenvane allocation, since the academy's file appeared to be short a lot and the clerk here — Seln wrote — was new and anxious and would be grateful. It was a lie of a very specific grade: false in its premise, true in every fact, and answerable in four lines by a man who would enjoy answering it.
    22	
    23	The answer came back in three days, which was faster than the post had any business being, and it was four lines, and the fourth line was the one he had paid for. The western stores had issued fourteen lots of the second run. The summaries accounted for thirteen.
    24	
