    67	
    68	The moment the case opened, the man holding it stopped being a keeper and became a witness. And witnesses did not retire.
    69	
    70	He had audited the files of enough witnesses to know their actuarial tables by heart, and the tables were not dramatic, which was what made them persuasive. Witnesses did not, in the main, come to violent ends; that was a broadside's understanding of the trade and it was wrong in the direction that flattered everybody. What happened to witnesses was that their circumstances became slowly and comprehensively unworkable. Employment ended for reasons the employer believed. Testimony was received and filed and never reached a seat. Corroboration was requested and the corroborating documents were found to be of uncertain provenance, which was true, because the man had taken them. And at the end of a process that no single person had authored, there was a man in a rented room in a town he had not chosen, holding papers nobody would receive, whose account of what had been done to him was indistinguishable from the ravings of a man who had gone wrong.
    71	
    72	He had written four such assessments. He had been correct in all four. Three of the four had also, he now believed, been the mechanism rather than the description of it, and he had understood that at the time and had gone on writing them, because the alternative had been to be the fifth.
    73	
    74	The catechism was correct. It had been correct for fifteen years. It was correct tonight.
    75	
    76	And he watched it lose.
    77	
    78	Without drama, the way everything about him happened. The catechism said: sealed. The audit said: every session the boy fights inside the instrument believing the instrument is merely broken; every filing the researcher perfects against a corridor she drew without knowing who built it; every night the honest men feed true reports into a mill that does not care where the river wanted to go — and now the paper has come to the gate, and the paper's next grade does not knock.
    79	
    80	The audit had been accruing against the catechism for two and a half years, a line at a time, and he could produce the lines, because producing lines was the only thing he had ever been good at.
    81	
    82	A boy who read every form at his counter, both sides, and asked about the seventh line, and said thank you for the answer.
    83	
    84	A night in a records office with a lamp and a sightline, and a boy handed a piece of another man's trade who had understood without being told twice that the debt was not the tradecraft but the exposure, and who had carried the debt unpaid and un-mentioned for two years rather than spend the thing that would have squared it.
    85	
    86	A table with a place at it that nobody had ever announced. He had counted, once, how many tables he had sat at in nineteen postings where the chair had been *his* rather than *a* chair, and the count was one, and the counting had annoyed him for a week.
    87	
    88	A Gold-tier match, in front of the whole continent, fought at the edge of what the boy had, with something held back and sealed the entire time to protect a cover whose owner had never been told it was being protected. He had worked that out afterward, alone, from the shape of what had not happened on that floor, and had sat with the finding for an hour, and had told nobody, and had not been thanked, and had not wanted to be, and had understood exactly what he had been handed and by whom.
    89	
    90	And on the fourth night the account closed, and the catechism lost, and the loss was quiet, and it was the largest event of his adult life.
    91	
