   124	The venue he selected was a records annex two floors below the library's north range — a room the assessment office controlled the allocation of, whose sightlines he had audited the week he arrived at Halcenvane, out of habit, against a need he had not then been able to name: one door, no windows, stone walls a voice did not carry through, and a corridor approach visible for forty feet in both directions from inside the doorway. There was a second thing about it he had noted in that first week and had never written down, which was that the corridor's forty feet ended at a service stair going up to the kitchen range, and that a person standing in the annex doorway would hear anyone coming down it about four seconds before seeing them, and that four seconds was enough.
