   139	
   140	Cael wrote the log the night before the annex with the residence quiet and the case — he had not seen where Seln kept it; he had not asked — somewhere on the campus below him, in a room or on a shelf, the most expensive object at Halcenvane and the least remarkable.
   141	
   142	*Fifteen years he's kept whatever it is. Nineteen postings. He carried it past a records office he was burgling with my help, through a tournament the whole continent watched, into a delegation coach and out of it, and never once let it be seen, and tonight he carried it across a campus to ask five minutes of my evening — and answered "you're sure" with "no," and told me the no was the proof.*
   143	
   144	*He's right. It is. I have spent four years learning to price certainty, and the men who are sure are always selling something. The man who is not sure, and comes anyway, carrying the thing that kept him alive, to hand it to the people it will endanger — that man is spending, not selling.*
