    35	He logged both. He logged that both had closed. He logged, in the flat voice the log kept for things he did not like, that *closing correctly on its own* was a phrase he had now written four times about an architecture nobody in the world could examine, and that he had no way of knowing what an incorrect closure would look like from the inside, and that this was the honest state of his knowledge and would remain so.
    36	
    37	Around him the rest of the road cut's bill was being paid on ordinary schedules by ordinary people. Lira's ankle went strapped for six days and she ran the Silver program's intervals on it on the fourth, badly, once, and was seen doing it by Karis and did not do it again. Brom's right shoulder kept its guarded carriage for most of a week, and he moved Karis's crates one-handed rather than mention it, and mentioned it exactly once, to Cael, in four words: "It'll be right by session." Karis's right hand came out of its dressing on the fifth day with the working edge of the first two fingers shiny and new and permanently a shade paler, which was a thing that had happened to that hand before and would happen again, and which she noted in her own record as *ignition-margin, fourth instance, healing normal* and did not otherwise remark upon. Cael's forearm scabbed from wrist to elbow and itched, and the ribs on that side filed their complaint properly on the first morning and withdrew it slowly over eight days, and he wrote down the withdrawal rate because he wrote down everything.
    38	
    39	---
    40	
    41	Karis documented all of it, because documenting it was her seat at this particular table, and her comparative data had become, across every acquisition since the first, the only longitudinal record of the architecture that existed anywhere.
    42	
    43	She brought it to him on the third afternoon, in her annex — hers; the one with the lock; the other annex would wait until the recovery cleared, per the floor schedule's revision — and she laid it out the way she laid out everything, which was in an order that was already an argument.
    44	
    45	"Eighth acquisition. Second Silver-or-above in two years." She had four charts on the table and she put them down in sequence and did not hurry. "The pattern is acceleration into heavier sources. Wind and Pressure were Copper-adjacent equivalents. Iron, Compression, Ember: Iron-grade. Shadow: Bronze. Then Storm at Gold, Anchor at Silver, eight months apart — and the recovery arithmetic is holding. Two days this time against nine weeks for Storm, and the difference is the source-grade differential, exactly as the model predicts."
    46	
    47	"Two days and a doorway."
    48	
    49	"Two days, a doorway, one uncommanded deployment lasting twelve seconds, and a self-terminating release." She wrote it into the margin as he said it, in the small hand, without looking up. "Which is data and goes in. The doorway is the most interesting thing that has happened to this architecture in a year and I want it understood that I mean that as a researcher and not as a friend." She finished the note. "As a friend it frightened me."
    50	
    51	"It frightened me."
    52	
    53	"Good. Then we're calibrated." She turned the third chart round so it faced him: the recovery intervals plotted against source grade, eight points, a curve bending the way she had said it bent. "Here is the finding, and I am going to deliver it carefully, because I don't like it. The architecture is getting better at digestion faster than the meals are getting heavier. Storm cost nine weeks. Anchor cost two days, and Anchor is one grade lower, and one grade of differential does not buy you a factor of thirty. Something else improved. I think it's you — the banking discipline, the idle-state work, four years of cataloguing — and I think if I'm right about that, it's the best news in this file. But I can't prove it's you rather than the architecture, and the difference matters enormously, because one of those two things has a person in it who can decide to stop."
    54	
    55	She set the chart down and squared it.
    56	
    57	"So. For now. I am entering *for now* in the record at my own insistence, and I want the reason entered with it, because a limit without its reason gets read as caution by whoever reads it next and it is not caution. The model has no ceiling in it. Not *a high ceiling*. None. Eight points on a curve, no upper bound derivable, no failure case observed, and no comparison class anywhere in the literature — and a model with no ceiling is a model, not a law. If the ninth is Gold-grade or above, this curve predicts a recovery I would describe as impossible, and the correct response to a model predicting an impossibility is not to believe the model."
    58	
    59	"Minuted," said Cael, from the chair by the window, where the doorframe had finally stopped raising its hand.
    60	
    61	"Minuted," she agreed, and wrote it, and then, still writing: "It's also the eighth entry in a register that four years ago had one line in it and a question mark. I have three unpublishable papers, no citations, no peers, and a research subject I would resign a ranking to keep studying." She capped the pen. "I want that on the record too, in the same hand, at the same weight. Nobody in the world is doing this work. I'm doing it in a locked room for an audience of four."
    62	
    63	---
    64	
    65	The birthday fell on the recovery's third day, and Lira instigated it against the term's darkening sky, and both were true at once, because both were.
    66	
    67	The residence common room, one evening — and the proceedings' documents stacked face-down on the far table by Lira's own arrangement, a border drawn in paper: not tonight. She had done it that afternoon, alone, and she had done it thoroughly: every folder, every copy-set, the challenge itself, the production-order returns, the response drafts, all of it squared and turned over and weighted with a book, so that the room contained the term and did not display it.
    68	
    69	Brom acquired food with quiet competence, dishes arriving without any order ever being heard placed — the fish and the bread and a honey-cake of the northern kind that had no business existing this far south of Norhold and existed anyway, because Brom had made an arrangement with the kitchens in the autumn and the kitchens, like everyone Brom made arrangements with, had kept it. He carried the tray in with his left arm doing most of the work and set it down and said nothing about the shoulder, and Lira, on the strapped ankle, took the second tray, and the two of them going carefully across a common room in opposite directions was the only acknowledgment either of them made all evening that anything had happened on a road.
    70	
    71	Ephram was invited.
    72	
    73	It was the first time anyone outside the circle had been at one of these evenings, and the invitation was itself a statement the evening did not underline: the cohort captain, arriving with a bottle and the harbor's lock-keeper song and no questions, taking the seat the configuration opened for him as though the configuration had always had it. He put the bottle down, looked at the face-down stacks on the far table for exactly as long as it took to understand them, and turned his back on them for the rest of the night, which was the most tactful thing anyone did all week.
    74	
    75	Nobody told him what the term was becoming. Everybody at the table knew he knew the shape of it — he read the postings; he had walked the last box-trip of another year beside another man; he was Ephram — and the evening's grammar held him without ceremony and completely.
    76	
    77	He earned his seat inside ten minutes by doing the thing he was for, which was talking about work. He had the cohort's winter figures and a grievance about the wing-three allocation that had been revised out from under him that morning by a note from the assessment office; he described the note as "the politest piece of nothing I've been handed all year" and Seln, at the table, agreed that the office's correspondence had declined in substance for some seasons, and Cael put his face into a cup of cider and kept it there. Ephram also had, and produced, a bout tally he had been keeping in his own hand of every partner Cael had worked with in wing three since the autumn, ninety-one entries, because he had noticed that nobody else was keeping one and thought somebody ought to. He handed it over as a birthday item and said, "It's not a present. It's a record. You like records."
    78	
    79	"It's a present," said Karis.
    80	
    81	"It's a record," said Ephram, and drank.
    82	
    83	Seln sat at the table like a man who had been sitting at tables all his life. He ate what he was given, said perhaps nine things all evening, three of them dry enough to make Lira laugh out loud, and paid attention to the room's two doors in a manner that only one person present recognized and would not have recognized a year ago. Only a reader of the room — and the boy who kept the room's ledger — knew that across the campus, in quarters the office allocated, a plain scuffed traveling case was already down from its shelf, and had been down for six days, waiting on a floor schedule's revision.
    84	
    85	Hesk's package had been held ready as always, and it was the size of a satchel because it was one.
    86	
    87	Travel-grade, instrument-maker's work: waxed leather over a frame built the way the man built everything, for forty years of weather; a strap stitched for a shoulder that would carry it far, with the stitching doubled at both anchor points and a spare buckle sewn flat into the underside of the strap where it would not chafe, because Hesk had never in his life given anyone a thing without its spare part. The log's exact dimensions accommodated in an interior pocket with a flap that closed against rain. A second pocket the width of a pen-case. Every seam oiled, every buckle brass, the whole object a tool whose meaning hid in plain sight and did not hide very hard.
    88	
    89	The note was in the interior pocket, in the upright letters:
    90	
    91	*Eighteen. A man's tools should fit the road he's actually on, not the one they promised him. Your grandmother would say I'm being unsubtle. She'd be right. Mind the shape. — H.*
    92	
    93	Cael read it twice at the table and passed it to Lira, per the family's open post, and she read it aloud, and the table sat with it for a moment — a border-town instrument-maker, five hundred miles west, who had been visited by two polite men about his shop's title and had answered with a satchel built for weather.
    94	
    95	Brom turned the satchel once in his big hands with a craftsman's son's assessment: the frame, the strap's anchor points, the flap's fall, the brass. "That's a ten-year bag." A beat. "He's telling you where the log's going to live."
    96	
    97	"He's telling him it already does," said Karis, which she had said once before about a different volume, and the rhyme was the family's own, and nobody remarked it, and everybody heard it.
    98	
    99	Lira, on the other side of the table with her foot up, had not said anything for a while, and the not-saying was doing work.
   100	
   101	She had run the Silver program's intervals for two months. She had a name in the wing-two book and an entry in the Norhold bracket-lists and a bout schedule for the spring that Karis had copied out and pinned inside the annex door, and on the road she had spent four bursts and a landing on a drainage channel's lip to break a perimeter's timing for somebody else's benefit, and the ankle underneath the strapping was going to cost her the spring schedule's first three entries whatever anyone said about it.
   102	
   103	"Say the number," Cael said, because it was her evening as much as his and she would not be the one to open it.
   104	
   105	"Three bouts." She said it flat, the way she said measurements. "Six weeks off the intervals. Which puts the Silver bracket qualification in the summer instead of the spring, which puts it after the ruling, which is the part nobody at this table wants to say out loud, so I've said it." She reached over and took a second piece of the cake. "And before anyone tells me it was worth it — I know. I did the arithmetic in the coach on the way home, twice, and it came out the same both times, and it wasn't close. That's not the point. The point is that I want the bracket, I've wanted it since I was ten, and I paid three bouts of it into a road cut in Ostrand province, and I'd like the ledger to show I noticed."
   106	
   107	"It shows," said Karis.
   108	
   109	"Good." Lira ate the cake. "Then that's the whole of it, and the paper's still face-down, and Ephram doesn't know what any of us are talking about, and it's your birthday."
   110	
   111	Ephram, who did not have the context for any of it, looked at the satchel with a captain's plain eye and supplied the only reading available to him, which was, as usual, most of the truth. "Whoever made that made it for somebody who's leaving." He said it without weight, cutting cake. "Good man. My father gave me boots."
   112	
   113	Nobody answered him. Lira refilled his cup, which was an answer.
   114	
   115	Seln, at the far end, looked at the strap's doubled stitching for slightly longer than a man looks at a strap, and said, to nobody, without lifting his eyes from the strap: "Two anchor points and a spare buckle sewn where it will not chafe. That is a maker who has watched a strap fail on a road and decided it would not happen to anyone he liked." He returned to his plate. "I would have liked to meet him."
   116	
   117	"You will," said Cael, and did not know, saying it, whether he was making a prediction or a promise, and entered it that night as neither.
   118	
   119	He was eighteen, witnessed — the date written into the log in his own hand at the table, per the custom now three birthdays old, the smallest of the three and, though only the term suspected it, the last that would ever be kept inside an institution's walls: *Eighteen. Written at Halcenvane, in the residence common room, third month of the winter term, ninth day. Witnesses: four of my own and a cohort captain who thinks he's here for cake.*
   120	
   121	The evening ran until the twelfth bell, and Cael kept his own count of it, because keeping the count was how he held a thing he wanted to keep.
   122	
   123	Six at the table and a seventh chair pulled up for the bottle. Two lamps. The fire down to a working red by the ninth bell. Forty minutes of the lock-keeper song and its arguments. One honey-cake, northern, cut by Lira into pieces of aggressively equal size, per a custom now a year old that nobody had ever named and everybody obeyed. Fifteen feet between the table they were at and the table where the term lay face-down under a book, and not one person crossing that fifteen feet all evening.
   124	
   125	He caught himself, somewhere around the tenth bell, doing what he did — running the room as an instrument, itemizing it, filing it — and made the decision he had learned at a scarred table by a harbor window to let himself make perhaps three times a year, and put the instrument down. No count. No log. He sat in a common room he was going to lose, with five people who knew both his ledgers and one who knew neither and had come anyway, and did nothing at all with any of it for the better part of an hour.
   126	
   127	The evening ran until the twelfth bell. Ephram sang the lock-keeper song and made Brom sing the second verse, which Brom did, in a bass that surprised nobody who had ever heard him breathe. Lira put her strapped foot on a chair and refused to explain it. Karis argued with Ephram for twenty minutes about whether a cohort tally constituted a record within the meaning of anything, and lost on the merits and said so. Seln left first, at the tenth bell, with a nod that took in the whole table, and at the door he stopped and turned back and said, to the room and not to anyone, "It is a good room. I have been in a great many." Then he went, and the door closed, and Lira looked at the door and then at Cael and did not say the thing she was thinking, because the paper on the far table was face-down and would stay face-down until morning.
   128	
   129	---
   130	
   131	The inventory went into the satchel's first use that night, at the window, the new leather's smell in the room and the fountain running below, and it was the fullest the log had ever carried:
   132	
   133	*Eight confirmed fragments. Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, deployment none, ever, anywhere; the seal's third year begins. Storm-adjacent — Daeva; settled, resettled twice this week around the new neighbor, holding. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; two days integrated; idle state learned; the doorways have stopped raising their hands, and I have entered in the deployment notes the first honest sentence about it: I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people, taken from a hand with no face, and I have not yet decided what it is for, and the not-deciding is entered as a decision.*
   134	
   135	*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Turned over once, per the custom at intervals that matter. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
   136	
   137	*One term, five sittings spent, one ruling pending, one road cut behind us, one case across the campus, down from its shelf.*
   138	
   139	*Eighteen. Eight fragments. Tomorrow, Seln's annex. Hesk says mind the shape. I think tomorrow is the shape.*
   140	
   141	He capped the pen and put the log into the pocket built for it and closed the flap, which closed against nothing, because it was not raining. Then he set the satchel on the sill and left it there, where the cold came through the glass and would keep the leather honest.
   142	
   143	It sat in the window's square with the fountain going under it: waxed, strapped, brass at every buckle, the spare one flat on the strap's underside where it would not chafe — the tool built for the road he was actually on, a shape that did not belong in a residence room and had never once pretended to. And what came back to him, looking at it, was not Hesk's note or Brom's ten years or the term's arithmetic. It was a man getting up from a table at the tenth bell and stopping at the door on his way out.
   144	
   145	*It is a good room. I have been in a great many.*

