     1	# Chapter 12 — The Keeper
     2	
     3	The escort order's forensics took Seln four days, and he conducted them the way he conducted everything, in the margins of a working week, invisibly, from the service side, and what they returned was the last input the fifteen-year audit required.
     4	
     5	He traced the paper first, because paper was traceable and people were not.
     6	
     7	Registry stock moved through a supply chain, and the chain was the most thoroughly documented object in the Compact's whole apparatus, for the reason that made it useful: an institution that governs by instrument must be able to prove that an instrument is its own. Three mills held the contract, on rotation, by run. The runs were numbered and the numbering was published. Binders took the sheets and cut them and pressed the watermark and delivered in lots against a schedule. The registry's own stores took the lots and issued against requisition, and every requisition carried an office, a quantity, a date, and an initial, and the requisition books were held for twenty years by rule and in practice forever, because nobody had ever been given the authority to destroy one.
     8	
     9	A man who had spent fifteen years filing requisitions knew the chain's every joint. He had filed three hundred of them himself. He had also, in another life, spent a year learning where the chain's joints did not close, which was a different education and had been paid for by a different employer.
    10	
    11	The escort order's stock was genuine. Current issue. He had it in his hands for nine seconds at the gate — Bracken's copy was a copy, and a copy carries none of the information that matters, so what he had was the nine seconds and the memory, which for Seln was a working document. Western stores. Second mill run of the year: the watermark's cross-bar sat two fingers' width from the sheet's short edge on the second run and a hair further out on the first, which was a difference of about a sixteenth of an inch and had been introduced by a binder's re-set in the spring and had cost the western district a memorandum.
    12	
    13	So: western stores, second run, lot issued between the spring and midsummer.
    14	
    15	That gave him a question with a finite answer, and finite answers were the only kind he pursued. Which office had drawn from that lot?
    16	
    17	He spent the first day at Halcenvane's own stores counter, where the assessment office's entitlement to read the ledger had never once been questioned, and read the district's issue summaries for the year, which came down to the academies quarterly and which nobody read. Six offices in the district had drawn from the western stores in the relevant months, all six for quantities consistent with their traffic, all six initialed, all six unremarkable.
    18	
    19	He spent the second day writing one letter.
    20	
    21	It went to a man in the western stores whom he had known in a posting twelve years before and had not corresponded with in nine, and it was three sentences about a requisition number that did not exist and a polite query as to whether the number belonged to the Halcenvane allocation, since the academy's file appeared to be short a lot and the clerk here — Seln wrote — was new and anxious and would be grateful. It was a lie of a very specific grade: false in its premise, true in every fact, and answerable in four lines by a man who would enjoy answering it.
    22	
    23	The answer came back in three days, which was faster than the post had any business being, and it was four lines, and the fourth line was the one he had paid for. The western stores had issued fourteen lots of the second run. The summaries accounted for thirteen.
    24	
    25	The fourteenth had gone out on a requisition slip that the stores held in its own file and that had never entered the district's issue summary at all — no office named, quantity entered, date entered, and in the space where the drawing office's designation went, a mark rather than a word.
    26	
    27	That was the third remove and it cost him the second of the two favors he had been keeping for something. He had kept them for eight years without knowing what for, which was the correct way to keep a favor, and he spent them both inside a week on a paper trail about a boy, and made the entry in no ledger anywhere, because the ledger for that was the one that could not be requisitioned.
    28	
    29	Stock that entered the chain and exited it without a requisition was stock that had been drawn at a level above the ledgers, and there was exactly one level above the ledgers, and he had run operations on stock like it, in another life, for the level in question.
    30	
    31	He knew which supply chain the paper had walked out of. He had walked paper out of it himself.
    32	
    33	---
    34	
    35	The rest of the forensics confirmed what the paper had already said, and he assembled them at the dark desk on the fourth evening in the order an audit takes its inputs, which is worst last.
    36	
    37	The pre-restriction inventory: five offices, complete, executed at a tempo that meant staffing, which meant budget, which meant authorization at an altitude no district controlled. He had run three inventories in his career and had never had more than two clerks for any of them, and two clerks had taken nine weeks for a man with a wife and a workshop. Five offices in six weeks against a delegation, an academy, three provincial registries and a border town was not a clerk's tempo. It was an office's.
    38	
    39	The escort order's probe: professionally conceived, professionally accepted in failure. That was the input he disliked most and he made himself sit with it longest. An amateur repeats a failed approach. A bureaucracy escalates a failed approach through its own grades, which takes months and generates paper. What had come to the gate had done neither. It had made one attempt, at a cost of four practitioners and a morning, had been refused by a chancellor and a registrar, had declined to demand the written refusal that any real office would have wanted more than the enrollee himself — and had ridden away at a working trot. An apparatus that prices its probes and does not repeat them is an apparatus with a schedule, and a schedule implies a next item, and the next item was not going to be paper.
    40	
    41	And the four grades of pressure that came next — he could name them, in order, because he had been the instrument of two of them and the analyst of the other two.
    42	
    43	The probe repeated somewhere without a Withrow. That was cheapest and nearly certain: the same paper, the same four people, presented at a waystation, a ferry landing, a meet steward's table, an inn yard on a road at the end of a long day — anywhere the person holding the door was a man with no registrar behind him and a strong preference for not being the one who obstructed the registry. It worked more often than not. He had seen it work on institutions with charters, and had once, in his fourth posting, watched it work on a magistrate.
    44	
    45	The interception, off-channel, deniable, timed to a schedule the target could not refuse. The elegance of it was the schedule: a respondent in proceedings must attend the proceedings, and attendance requires a road, and a road is the one place a person's movements are published in advance by the very instrument compelling them. He had planned two of these. Both had gone as planned, which he had been proud of at the time in the way a man is proud of a thing he has not yet finished thinking about.
    46	
    47	The custody that became paperwork by the time anyone asked. This was the grade that had ended two careers he knew of and had never once been recorded as anything but an administrative irregularity. A person is taken; a facility receives him; a file is opened at the facility with a lawful heading; within nine days there is an instrument on record that authorizes what has already happened, and the instrument is genuine, and the office that issued it exists by then, and every question thereafter is answered accurately.
    48	
    49	And the last grade, which had no name in any code and which his trade had called, in its own dry grammar, *resolution*. He had never been its instrument. He had been its analyst twice, which meant he had read the product afterward and had written assessments of it in the register the office used, and he could still recite one of his own sentences from a report nine years old, and the reciting was the closest thing to a wound he had.
    50	
    51	Against all of it, the boy's sentence from the council, quoted in Seln's memory with professional distaste every day since: *the text still works.*
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	He walked his logic one last time on the fourth night, at the dark desk, because the logic had kept him alive for fifteen years and deserved the courtesy of a final hearing.
    56	
    57	The operative's catechism, learned in his first posting, proved in all nineteen: insurance is only insurance while it stays sealed.
    58	
    59	He had been taught it as four lines by a woman in her sixties who ran the western training and had made him say them back, and he had said them back for a fortnight until she stopped asking, and he had never needed to look at them again.
    60	
    61	*What you carry is worth what it has not done.*
    62	*A thing kept is a wall. A thing spent is a door, and doors open both ways.*
    63	*The keeper is invisible. The witness is a location.*
    64	*Nobody has ever protected a man by believing him.*
    65	
    66	The case's whole value — to him, to anyone — lay in its being closed. A keeper with a sealed case was a man with a price on his silence that no one knew they were paying, which was the only kind of price that stayed paid. Fifteen years of it had bought him nineteen postings, a clean separation from a service that did not do clean separations, and a desk at a mid-tier academy where nobody had ever asked him a question he could not answer flatly.
    67	
    68	The moment the case opened, the man holding it stopped being a keeper and became a witness. And witnesses did not retire.
    69	
    70	He had audited the files of enough witnesses to know their actuarial tables by heart, and the tables were not dramatic, which was what made them persuasive. Witnesses did not, in the main, come to violent ends; that was a broadside's understanding of the trade and it was wrong in the direction that flattered everybody. What happened to witnesses was that their circumstances became slowly and comprehensively unworkable. Employment ended for reasons the employer believed. Testimony was received and filed and never reached a seat. Corroboration was requested and the corroborating documents were found to be of uncertain provenance, which was true, because the man had taken them. And at the end of a process that no single person had authored, there was a man in a rented room in a town he had not chosen, holding papers nobody would receive, whose account of what had been done to him was indistinguishable from the ravings of a man who had gone wrong.
    71	
    72	He had written four such assessments. He had been correct in all four. Three of the four had also, he now believed, been the mechanism rather than the description of it, and he had understood that at the time and had gone on writing them, because the alternative had been to be the fifth.
    73	
    74	The catechism was correct. It had been correct for fifteen years. It was correct tonight.
    75	
    76	And he watched it lose.
    77	
    78	Without drama, the way everything about him happened. The catechism said: sealed. The audit said: every session the boy fights inside the instrument believing the instrument is merely broken; every filing the researcher perfects against a corridor she drew without knowing who built it; every night the honest men feed true reports into a mill that does not care where the river wanted to go — and now the paper has come to the gate, and the paper's next grade does not knock.
    79	
    80	The audit had been accruing against the catechism for two and a half years, a line at a time, and he could produce the lines, because producing lines was the only thing he had ever been good at.
    81	
    82	A boy who read every form at his counter, both sides, and asked about the seventh line, and said thank you for the answer.
    83	
    84	A night in a records office with a lamp and a sightline, and a boy handed a piece of another man's trade who had understood without being told twice that the debt was not the tradecraft but the exposure, and who had carried the debt unpaid and un-mentioned for two years rather than spend the thing that would have squared it.
    85	
    86	A table with a place at it that nobody had ever announced. He had counted, once, how many tables he had sat at in nineteen postings where the chair had been *his* rather than *a* chair, and the count was one, and the counting had annoyed him for a week.
    87	
    88	A Gold-tier match, in front of the whole continent, fought at the edge of what the boy had, with something held back and sealed the entire time to protect a cover whose owner had never been told it was being protected. He had worked that out afterward, alone, from the shape of what had not happened on that floor, and had sat with the finding for an hour, and had told nobody, and had not been thanked, and had not wanted to be, and had understood exactly what he had been handed and by whom.
    89	
    90	And on the fourth night the account closed, and the catechism lost, and the loss was quiet, and it was the largest event of his adult life.
    91	
    92	He noted, in the audit-voice, that the catechism had not been refuted. That mattered to him and he made himself write it, in the archive that could not be requisitioned, in the same flat register he would have used for a stores discrepancy: *the four lines remain true. A witness is still a location. Nothing about the arithmetic has changed. What has changed is that I have stopped being willing to be the man the arithmetic protects.*
    93	
    94	Then he sat for twenty minutes doing nothing at all, which was the longest he had sat doing nothing since he was thirty, and then he stood up and put the lamp out.
    95	
    96	---
    97	
    98	He took the case down.
    99	
   100	It came off the shelf in his quarters the way it had come off nineteen shelves in nineteen postings — a plain locked traveling case, scuffed at the corners, the least remarkable object he owned, which was its whole design. It weighed eleven pounds. It had a maker's stamp inside the lid that belonged to a firm that had gone out of business twenty years ago, which was the second-best thing about it, and no maker's mark anywhere on the outside, which was the first. He had carried it through customs at two border stations, through a fire at an inn, and past a records office he had been burgling, and had never once had to explain it, because a mild administrative man with a case is not a question anyone forms.
   101	
   102	He did not open it.
   103	
   104	He carried it, for the first time in fifteen years, out of his own quarters and across the campus.
   105	
   106	The covered walk had its evening traffic on it: third-years going down to the refectory in twos, a steward with a barrow, the lamp-man doing the north range, two of Rooke's cohort arguing about a hip. The fountain court had the fountain running because Withrow had ruled in her second year that a fountain that is only run for visitors is a lie about the institution. It was twelve minutes' walk. He took it at his own pace, which was the pace he took everything, and he passed twenty-three people, and he counted them, and not one of them looked at the case, because the case was furniture and so was he, and both facts had been built deliberately over fifteen years and were being spent tonight, together, in one direction.
   107	
   108	He requested five minutes of Cael's evening at the residence stair.
   109	
   110	The conversation was five sentences long.
   111	
   112	"You said the text still works." Seln stood at the stair's foot with the case in his hand, held the way clerks held cases, and his voice was the counter voice, and his face was not. "I need to show you whose text it is. Not here — all five of us, somewhere I choose, this week."
   113	
   114	Cael had been reading two and a half years of this man, and knew the case on sight the way a person knows the one sealed thing in a house he has lived in: "You're sure."
   115	
   116	"No," said Seln. "That's how you'll know it's true."
   117	
   118	Then he inclined his head half an inch, and went back up the walk with the case, and Cael stood on the stair with his hand on the rail for a while afterward, doing the thing he did instead of feeling things where anyone could see him, which was counting: twelve minutes' walk each way, twenty-four minutes, for five sentences, one of which was a no.
   119	
   120	---
   121	
   122	The arrangements ran in tradecraft, because tradecraft was the only language the errand could safely be spoken in.
   123	
   124	The venue he selected was a records annex two floors below the library's north range — a room the assessment office controlled the allocation of, whose sightlines he had audited the week he arrived at Halcenvane, out of habit, against a need he had not then been able to name: one door, no windows, stone walls a voice did not carry through, and a corridor approach visible for forty feet in both directions from inside the doorway. There was a second thing about it he had noted in that first week and had never written down, which was that the corridor's forty feet ended at a service stair going up to the kitchen range, and that a person standing in the annex doorway would hear anyone coming down it about four seconds before seeing them, and that four seconds was enough.
   125	
   126	He manufactured the schedule gap himself: a floor-inventory session, entered in the office's own calendar in the office's own hand, annex closed for cataloguing, the entry so ordinary that the office's other clerk initialed it without reading it. It was the ninth such entry he had made that term. The other eight had been genuine floor inventories, conducted, minuted, and filed, and he had made them for a year and a half for no reason except that a pattern is only cover if it existed before you needed it.
   127	
   128	The six would arrive separately, by three routes, across an hour. He issued the instructions at the residence table in the form of a floor schedule — a single sheet, ruled, headed *supervised floor allocation, remaining term*, with times against names and a column of room designations — and the room designations were the routes and the times were the times, and any person who picked the sheet up would have read a floor schedule, because it was one; the allocations were real and he had entered them in the office's system that afternoon. It was the best cipher he had ever used, because it was not one.
   129	
   130	Karis worked it out in nine seconds and said nothing. Lira worked it out when Brom said "why am I on a floor at the sixth bell, I don't train at the sixth bell," and Karis said "you do this week," and Brom looked at the sheet again and said "ah," and folded it, and that was the whole of the operational briefing.
   131	
   132	And the null report went north that week, one last time, in the old pattern, because the pattern's breaking had to happen on his schedule and not the pattern's.
   133	
   134	He drafted it at the counter in the ordinary morning, with the counter's lamps lit and two enrollees waiting behind the rail for floor-time stamps, and he stamped both of them in the middle of drafting it, because a document written in one sitting behind a closed door is a document that looks different from the four hundred and nine before it. It went in the office's standard form, in the register fifteen years had polished to instrument-grade: *subject's activity: routine; enrollment defense conducted per schedule; no undisclosed capability observed; term proceeds.* True. Complete in form. Empty.
   135	
   136	He read it once — the office's finest work, the genre he had invented and would not miss — and filed it by the evening dispatch, and the filing was the last document of its kind, and he knew it, and the knowing appeared nowhere on the form, which was the point of the form, and always had been.
   137	
   138	---
   139	
   140	Cael wrote the log the night before the annex with the residence quiet and the case — he had not seen where Seln kept it; he had not asked — somewhere on the campus below him, in a room or on a shelf, the most expensive object at Halcenvane and the least remarkable.
   141	
   142	*Fifteen years he's kept whatever it is. Nineteen postings. He carried it past a records office he was burgling with my help, through a tournament the whole continent watched, into a delegation coach and out of it, and never once let it be seen, and tonight he carried it across a campus to ask five minutes of my evening — and answered "you're sure" with "no," and told me the no was the proof.*
   143	
   144	*He's right. It is. I have spent four years learning to price certainty, and the men who are sure are always selling something. The man who is not sure, and comes anyway, carrying the thing that kept him alive, to hand it to the people it will endanger — that man is spending, not selling.*
   145	
   146	*Whatever's in that case, it's about to become the most expensive thing anyone has ever handed me. Sleep. Read in the morning.*
   147	
   148	---
   149	
   150	Havel added the sixth entry the same week, at Ostrand, late, in his quarters above the records office, and he did not know he was converging with anything.
   151	
   152	The proceedings' exhibits had kept him at the ledger past the fourth bell — production orders logged, the confluence meet's served demand entered, the response's supplementary filings indexed, twenty-two evaluator names read into a minute and now to be cross-referenced against the district's own officer rolls, which took an hour and which he did correctly. The office below was empty by the second bell. He worked in it alone four nights a week, and had done at three postings, and had never once been asked to.
   153	
   154	The evening's last item had been a routing slip he was not required to read and read anyway, because reading was the flaw his whole quiet career had been built around: a facilities office's constitution instrument, filed at the district seat, correct in format, signed under a seal he could not match to any office in the seat's own registry of seals.
   155	
   156	He checked the registry twice. The registry of seals was two volumes and an appendix, held at the records office counter, updated by his own hand four times a year, and he knew its structure better than any living person because he had recopied the appendix in his first posting when the binding failed. He went through the current volume, and then the appendix, and then — because the honest procedure required it and because he was the only person who would ever know whether he had done it — the superseded volume, in case the office was old and had been reconstituted under a prior designation.
   157	
   158	It was not there. He was a records officer; checking registries was the whole of his authority; and the registry of seals did not contain the seal that had constituted the office whose paper was moving through his proceedings.
   159	
   160	He sat with that for a while, at a ledger, in an empty office, at the fifth bell.
   161	
   162	Then he logged the routing slip per procedure — accurately, completely, with the finding nowhere in it, because a records officer's log is not the place for a records officer's opinion and Havel had never in four years confused the two. He banked the lamp, locked the counter, and went up to his quarters, and took the private note from the kit where it had traveled since Norhold — five entries, four years, a file that was not a file about a designation that had no author — and read the five before he wrote the sixth, which he did every time, and which had begun as diligence and had become something he did not have a word for.
   163	
   164	Then he added the sixth, in his careful hand: the date, the office's designation, the seal's format, and the finding a junior records officer was permitted to make to no one: *a second instrument in this matter now exists whose authorizing authority cannot be identified from any registry I can reach. The first has stood for four years. Nobody has ever answered for it. I am keeping this note because the registries are supposed to answer, and they don't, and someone should be writing that down.*
   165	
   166	Carrier intact. Unknowing. Converging.

