   166	Carrier intact. Unknowing. Converging.
     1	# Chapter 13 — The Ostrand Road
     2	
     3	The annex was set for the morning after the documentary session, because the proceedings' schedule intervened first — a sitting at Ostrand requiring the enrollee's presence for the production order's return — and the delegation coach took the river road it had taken all term, at first light, with the five of them in it and the term's fifth trip worn smooth.
     4	
     5	The trip had a shape by now, and Cael could have written it out in order without looking up: the bluff gate at the fifth bell; the descent to the ferry in the dark, the ferryman's two lanterns and his one remark about the water; the crossing, nine minutes, always nine; the water-meadows for two hours in the grey, flat and reeded and empty except for the winter geese; the hamlet with the smithy that was never open; and then the road's long cut through the river bluffs, two hours short of the city, where the bluffs came down to meet the water and the road went through them instead of around.
     6	
     7	He knew the cut better than he knew most rooms at Halcenvane. Four hundred and forty yards of it, a quarter mile, running east toward Ostrand between banks that rose on both sides — the north bank shallow enough to climb, gorse and loose scree, three places along its run where a man could get up it without hands; the south bank the cut face of the bluff itself, stone, undercut at its foot for eight or ten yards near the midpoint where two centuries of winter water had eaten it back. The roadway forty feet wide between the banks' feet, cambered to the crown, a drainage channel a foot deep along the northern side and running now with the winter's melt. A culvert mouth and a mile-marker two hundred yards in from the western mouth. No branch road, no field gate, no way off the road's line for a quarter mile except up.
     8	
     9	He charted the cut the way he charted everything the coach passed, out of habit four years deep, and the habit was why he felt it before the coach entered it.
    10	
    11	Pressure, wrong, across the cut's mouth — not the medium folding; nothing so loud — a *settledness*, fixed points blooming in a geometry the Iron-adjacent read mapped in real time as the coach closed: anchored positions, laid across the road's width and up both banks, six of them, then nine, a lattice assembling with the unhurried precision of a craft that had chosen its ground in advance. He had felt the signature once before, from a gallery above a gate, folded down into a single woman's placement. This was the same signature at working scale, and it was already built, and the coach was driving into it.
    12	
    13	"Stop the coach," he said, and the driver — Halcenvane's own man, two years on this road — stopped the coach, and the road's quarter mile held, for one long breath, exactly the stillness of a floor before flags.
    14	
    15	The coach stood thirty yards inside the western mouth, on the crown, its horses' heads already coming round against the bit because horses read fixed points too, in their own vocabulary. Cael counted the lattice out loud while he counted it inwardly, because the others could not see it and everything he could see belonged to all of them: nine anchors, then fourteen — four along the northern bank's shoulder, five along the southern shelf above the undercut, four across the roadway itself in a shallow arc opening westward, and one at the arc's focus, on the centerline, forty feet ahead. Not a wall. A funnel with its mouth toward them and its throat at the specialist's feet, and behind her, closing the cut's eastern half, nothing at all — because nothing was needed there. The whole design assumed the coach would come on, and the road would narrow, and the road would end.
    16	
    17	"Fourteen points," he said. "Four on the road. She's got the crown."
    18	
    19	They came down the banks on both sides, six of them, in traveling grey with no livery and no paper, at the pace of professionals who had nowhere to be because the place they needed to be was already theirs. No declarations. No orders read at any gate. Two came down the northern scree behind the coach, west of it, cutting the road back toward the ferry; two came off the southern shelf ahead and to the right; one walked out onto the roadway from the culvert's shadow, where he had been for some time. And the Anchor specialist — her; the compact unhurried woman from the yard, unmistakable at any distance to the instrument that had charted her — stood at the lattice's keystone on the road's centerline, forty feet ahead, having laid her geometry across the cut *before* the coach entered it.
    20	
    21	The purpose of the whole arrangement was transparent, and Cael stated it to the coach in the flat voice the moment required, because everyone deserved the arithmetic before the arithmetic moved.
    22	
    23	"Custody. Not assault — they want a detention that's paperwork by the time anyone asks. No Withrow here. No gate to log it." He was already out of the coach, because the one thing custody could not survive was its subjects choosing the ground. "They've priced everything except what we do next."
    24	
    25	"How long have they been here?" Karis said, coming out after him with the case still in her hand and setting it down on the step, which was the only concession to hurry she made all morning.
    26	
    27	"The lattice was finished before we came round the bend. An hour. Maybe two."
    28	
    29	"Then they knew the schedule." She said it as a note taken, and Cael heard it entered. "That's the fact worth carrying out of here. Everything else is a road."
    30	
    31	Lira had gone to the coach's off side and come back along it and was looking east, at the four road anchors and the woman behind them, with her head very slightly tilted. "They've left the eastern half open."
    32	
