    57	---
    58	
    59	The Anchor specialist worked him toward the cut's southern face across four sustained exchanges, and the four exchanges were the hardest floor-craft of his life after one storm.
    60	
    61	**First exchange.** She let him come. That was the whole of it, and it took twelve seconds and taught him more than the next thirty: he moved off the crown toward her at an angle that opened the road's northern shoulder behind him, and she did not close the shoulder, and did not step, and did not declare, and the shoulder shut anyway — a new point blooming at the culvert's mouth, twelve feet behind his left heel, laid there while he was still committing to the step that made it worth laying. Her lattice was not an attack. It was *subtraction*: exits priced and then removed, one at a time, at the rate he generated them. He arrived at fifteen feet from her with the western half of the cut no longer available and understood that he had bought the distance with the only thing he had that she wanted, which was ground.
    62	
    63	**Second exchange.** He tested the pull. Wind at rate, off the crown to the southern shoulder and back, three crossings inside four seconds to make her spend points at speed and to price what a point cost him to cross — and she spent them, and it cost him more each time, and the third crossing came in a half-beat late because the second crossing had left a residue in the leg that had made it. That was the exchange's information, and the information was bad: the fixed points did not merely resist, they *accumulated*, and a suite built on repeated fast reversals was a suite paying interest. He came out of it eight feet nearer the southern face than he had gone in, which he had not chosen, and with the northern shoulder gone, which he had. He spent Pressure at combat load to break the third pull and it worked, and buying a thing that worked and arriving worse off than before was a sentence he would write down that evening in almost those words.
    64	
    65	**Third exchange.** She closed the road. Four points laid in eight seconds across the forty feet in a shallow arc whose focus was the undercut's stone lip, and the arc's courses ran through every line his footwork proposed, and his footwork proposed six of them and the read priced all six as dead before his weight had shifted. He took the only line that was not dead, which was forward, at her, at contact range, because a specialist standing on her own keystone was the one place in the geometry where a new point could not be laid without unmaking the geometry — and she gave him the contact, and took his forearm off her guard with a turn no wider than a door opening, and set an anchor against his own committed weight and let the weight arrive at it. He hit the roadway on his right side, and the cambered stone took the skin off his left forearm from wrist to elbow, and the ribs on that side registered a complaint they would file properly in the morning. He was up in a second and a half. The second and a half was hers, and she used it to lay two more.
    66	
