    11	The prior architectures had wanted things he understood. Wind wanted motion — it sat under a walk and made the walk want to be a run. Ember wanted contact, and had spent three days after its arrival making every surface in a room feel like a thing that could be asked. Storm had wanted, heavily, for nine weeks, the medium itself: the air in a corridor presenting as country, folds proposing themselves along every draft. All of those were appetites he could name, and a named appetite could be scheduled around.
    12	
    13	The Anchor-adjacent wanted *fixity*.
    14	
    15	It wanted to fix points, and it did not much care in what. For two days the doorways of the residence went subtly wrong at the edges of his attention — thresholds presenting themselves to some new sense as *anchorable*, lines of the room's geometry offering to be bound: the window's frame, the stair's turn at the second landing, the gap between the table and the wall, the four corners of a washstand, the exact place where the door's shadow crossed the floorboard's seam. Every aperture in his ordinary world raising its hand. It was not painful and it was not loud. It was worse than either, because it was *reasonable* — the component did not shout at him, it made small courteous proposals, at the rate of perhaps forty an hour, each one of them a place that could be held, and the whole difficulty of it was that a thing which proposes forty times an hour and is never wrong about the geometry is a thing you begin to agree with.
    16	
    17	He agreed with it once, on the second morning, and that was the incident that went into the record.
    18	
    19	He was crossing to the window with a cup in his hand — a thing he had done in that room several thousand times — and the doorway behind him raised its hand, and some part of him that had not been consulted said *yes*, and the doorway went fixed. Not dramatically. There was no sound. The frame simply stopped being a frame and became a held position, the way the road anchors had held, and Cael, four feet past it, felt the whole room reorganize itself around a point he had set without deciding to and had no idea how to unset, and stood very still with a cup in his hand for twelve seconds while the architecture ran the question *what did you just do* through every framework he owned and got no answer from any of them.
    20	
    21	It let go on its own. That was the part he wrote down twice. It let go on its own after twelve seconds — spent, the way a spent position releases — and the frame was a frame, and nothing in the room had changed except that a boy who had spent four years insisting his architecture was an instrument had just been given a demonstration of what it was like when the instrument moved first.
    22	
    23	He put the cup down. He got the log. He started cataloguing, hourly, in writing, the way he had learned Shadow's cost and Storm's weight: what the component proposed, how often, at what, and what it felt like in the half-second before the proposal — and by the second evening the strangeness had a grammar, and by the third morning the grammar had an idle state, which was the whole object of the exercise. The idle state was this: the component wanted fixity the way a hand wants a rail on a stair. It proposed. It did not insist. It went quiet the moment it was *named*, and it stayed quiet as long as the naming continued, and the naming was cheap and constant and had to be done for the rest of his life.
    24	
    25	The Storm-adjacent framework, heaviest neighbor, resettled twice — the whole architecture shifting a hair around the new mass, the loaded shelf's arithmetic run again, nine weeks of the winter's settling briefly reopened. The first resettling came in the small hours of the second night and lasted forty minutes and took the Wind economy with it, so that he stood up to cross the room and the room's floor arrived a half-beat late under a foot that had been counting on it. The second came on the third morning, was smaller, and closed correctly on its own inside a quarter of an hour.
    26	
    27	Brom sat with him through the first resettling, because somebody had, every time, since the second fragment, and the rota was a thing the four of them ran without ever having discussed it aloud. He came in at the turn of the second night with a chair and a lamp turned down and a book he did not open, and put the chair by the door where he could see both the bed and the corridor, and stayed forty minutes, which was the length of the resettling, and Cael was never sure afterward whether that was a coincidence.
    28	
    29	He said two things in the forty minutes.
