   101	He had believed the last line more than the other three together. It was the one that had kept him clean. You did not protect a subject by believing him; you protected him by counting what he did, and filing it true, and letting the true thing be your only argument. He had never believed anybody in his life. He had counted them.
   102	
   103	He had counted the boy for a year. Two slips on a common-room fire. A season of figures that never once settled into a line. An afternoon at Norhold where everything held back went out in one breath, on purpose, in front of eight thousand people. He had counted all of it, and filed none of it, and somewhere in the counting, without asking his leave, it had turned into the other thing.
   104	
   105	He believed him.
   106	
   107	*Nobody has ever protected a man by believing him.* It was true. He had never seen it done. He was going to try.
   108	
   109	The wing's clock went on with its slow tick over the counter. Nothing in the room changed its note. Seln stood with his two hands flat on the copying table and understood, quite calmly, that this was the largest thing that had happened to him since a green corridor twelve years ago, and that it had happened on a weekday morning between the post and the noon bell, and that nobody would ever write it down.
   110	
   111	He reached up and took the case off the shelf.
   112	
   113	---
   114	
   115	He carried it across the court at dusk, in the rain, with his coat buttoned over the top of it.
   116	
   117	He did not hurry and he did not take the covered walk. A man on the covered walk was a man out of the rain, and people looked at him in a vague friendly way as they looked at anybody sharing a dry place. A man crossing the open court in a downpour was a man with an errand, and nobody looked at a man with an errand.
   118	
   119	He counted them anyway, because he could not help it.
   120	
   121	A porter with a hand-cart of coal under an oilcloth: one. Two first-years running for the refectory with a coat held over both their heads: two, three. The fire-watch boy with his ladder: four. A Magister of the Ash faculty under an umbrella the size of a cart-wheel, who said, "Filthy, filthy," to nobody: five. The lodge-man's boy, sent across with a lamp: six.
   122	
   123	At the residence door a knot of third-years was standing out of the wet arguing about a bout, and he went round the edge of them: seven to twelve. On the stair, coming down, the Current girl from Rooke's program with her wet hair in a towel, who flattened herself to the wall to let him by and said, "Sorry, sorry," as if the stair were hers to be sorry about: thirteen. Two more on the first landing. Three in the corridor, one of whom was the captain, Ephram, with his green-taped file under his arm, who nodded to him and went on: eighteen. Five more behind half-open doors as he passed, reading or mending or asleep.
   124	
   125	Twenty-three. Not one of them looked at the thing under his coat. Why would they? The wing's assistant was going up a residence stair with something for somebody. It was the most ordinary sight on the hill.
   126	
   127	The boy's door was the last on the second corridor. Seln knocked once, and opened it when the boy said to.
   128	
   129	He was at the window, at the sill, with Hesk's book open and the lamp lit and the notice still pinned to the frame. He looked round, and his face did what it always did when a door opened, which was nothing, and then it went still in a different way.
   130	
   131	Seln unbuttoned his coat and set the case on the sill beside the book.
   132	
   133	It was not large. It was the size of a bound register, of dark leather gone pale at the corners, with a brass lock and no mark on the outside anywhere. Seln kept one hand on the lid and did not sit.
   134	
   135	"I'm going to open this," he said. "Not tonight."
   136	
   137	The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
   138	
   139	"You're sure."
   140	
   141	Seln heard what the boy was offering him. It was a way back down the stair. The boy had built it in two words and held it open, so that a man could take his case and go and nobody need ever mention it again. Seln looked at it, and did not take it.
   142	
   143	"No. That's how you'll know it's true."
   144	
   145	They decided the rest in a quarter of an hour, standing, with the case between them on the sill and the rain going on.
   146	
   147	Not here, said Seln. A residence room had a door anybody could knock on, and a corridor full of people who knew whose room it was. Not the wing either. The wing was his, and everybody who watched the wing knew it was his, and a lamp in it after hours was already a thing somebody wrote down.
   148	
   149	"A room with a lock," he said. "A name on the card that isn't yours or mine. A reason for people to go up to it one at a time without anybody thinking it's strange. Somewhere a person has to be on purpose."
   150	
   151	The boy thought about it for perhaps three breaths. "The reading room."
   152	
   153	"The researcher's."
   154	
   155	"It has Bracken's card on the door. *Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.* It has the brass lock and one key, and she wears the key. The annex across the landing has the code on a shelf and the assistant's table, and people go up and down that stair all day with books." He stopped. "And it's hers. Nobody on the hill would think twice about four people going up to her room. They've been doing it all year."
   156	
   157	"Five people," said Seln.
   158	
   159	"Five."
   160	
   161	"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
   162	
   163	The boy nodded.
   164	
   165	"I'll put it in the floor schedule," said Seln.
   166	
   167	He saw the boy not understand that, and then understand it, which took about as long as it took the rain to run from the top of the window to the sill.
   168	
   169	The wing posted the floor schedule every Seventh-day for the week to come: four floors, their hours, and who had booked each hour, in Seln's fair copy, pinned up on the wing's board and the residence board and the refectory door. Everybody on the hill read it. Nobody on the hill read it for anything but their own name.
   170	
   171	"Five lines on the First-day," said Seln. "Five floors, five hours, a quarter apart, each in one of your names. A practice booking. It's the most ordinary thing on the board. Read your own line. The floor means nothing. The hour is when you come up the stair."
   172	
   173	The boy took the pencil from the sill and wrote something on the back of his hand, and looked at it, and rubbed it out with his thumb.
   174	
   175	"There'll be six names in the First-day column," he said. "There always are. Ephram has the Crown yard from the second bell every First-day."
   176	
   177	"Five," said Seln. "The sixth is the captain, and he wants the floor."
   178	
   179	The boy almost smiled. Then he did not, and looked at the case again.
   180	
   181	---
   182	
   183	Seln wrote his weekly return that night at the copying table, in the old pattern, as he had written one every week for fifteen years.
   184	
   185	*Subject attended to his course of study. Subject attended the seat's fourth sitting as directed. An instrument presented at the house's gate on the Sixth-day was declined by the house; subject was not seen at the refusal. Subject's floor work within the band of the year. Nothing further to report.*
   186	
   187	Every word of it was true. He read it over in the voice he kept for the audit, and the voice found nothing wrong with it, which was the point and always had been. He folded it in the old way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold and the posting's mark in the corner, and put it in the satchel for the quick bag in the morning.
   188	
   189	Then he made the duplicate, as he always did, word for word, and opened the cupboard under the copying table and put it into the thickest bundle on the end of the row, the one with the name of this hill on its label.
   190	
   191	It was not the last of its kind. He knew that while he was tying the tape. He would write another next week, and the one after, and every week until the year closed, true and empty, in the old pattern, because the pattern was what the office expected and a pattern that changed was a thing somebody wrote down. Nothing in his life was going to look different from the outside. That was the other half of the catechism, the half she had never needed to say aloud.
   192	
   193	He put the case back on the shelf above the copying table, where it had stood for two years and where nobody had ever once looked for it, and blew out the lamp.
   194	
   195	---
   196	
   197	Bracken saw the chain go across Ostrand bridge a little after the fourth bell on the Fourth-day, and came down from his rooms with the ship's glass still in his hand to say so.
   198	
   199	He found Cael on the north wall, because it was where Cael always was at that hour, and stood beside him in the rain without an umbrella and gave him the glass. Cael found the bridge. The bridge-ward was at the near end in his oilskin, with a man from the town, hauling a chain across the roadway from post to post. Below them the river was standing up against the middle pier in a long brown hump, and the iron rings let into the stone were gone under it, all but the top two.
   200	
   201	"The third ring," said Bracken. "They shut it to everybody at the third. Carts, horses, people on foot. Since the year the middle arch went, which was before my time." He took the glass back. "The ferryman tied up at noon. I watched him do it."
   202	
   203	"The sitting's tomorrow."
   204	
   205	"The sitting's tomorrow," Bracken agreed. "The seat does not adjourn for weather. I have never known it to, and the house has never in my time asked it to." He looked out at the water for a moment longer. "Come and see the coachman. He's in the stable passage with a map and an opinion."
   206	
   207	The house's coachman had driven the delegation coach for as long as Cael had been on the hill. He was a short broad man with grey whiskers who talked to his horses in a low steady voice all day and to people hardly at all. He had a road map of the valley spread on an upturned bucket in the stable passage, weighted at the corners with four horseshoes, and he put one thick finger on it when they came in, as if he had been waiting with it there.
   208	
   209	"Up this side," he said. "West, along the bank road. Two hours if the bank road holds, and it will, it's high. To the upper ferry at the narrows." The finger moved. "She runs in any water. Rock both sides, no width for the river to throw her about in. Over there. Then back east down the river road on the far side, two hours more, into the city by the west gate." He looked up at Bracken. "Leave at the hour before light. In by noon. Sitting's after."
   210	
   211	"Thank you," said Bracken.
   212	
   213	The coachman folded the map along its old folds and put the horseshoes in his pocket and went back to his horses.
   214	
   215	That evening, in the refectory, Withrow came to the end of their table on her way out and stood behind Karis's chair with one hand on its back.
   216	
   217	"Since the gate, no enrollee of this house leaves the hill alone," she said. "You know that. Tomorrow you are five, and the house's counsel went down to her rooms by the wharf this afternoon while the bridge was still open, and will meet you at the side door." She looked along the table at each of them, the way she looked at a page before she signed it. "I would go with you, if the seat had asked for me. It has not. It has asked for him, and the maker of the copies, and the advocate of record. Go the long way and come back the short one."
   218	
   219	She went out.
   220	
   221	"The long way," said Lira, to her plate.
   222	
   223	"It's only a road," said Brom.
   224	
   225	He came up to Cael's room an hour later with his kit bag, all the same, and turned it out on the bed.
   226	
   227	It was what he took to every floor that was not the house's own. Two rolls of linen strapping, a roll of the stiff kind that the healers used for wrists, a tin of the porter's horse salve that smelled of tar and worked better than anything the infirmary had, a clean neckcloth, a flask, a heel of bread wrapped in a napkin, and a short length of leather with a buckle at each end that Cael had never seen him use and had never asked about. He went through all of it with his big careful fingers, as he did before a meet, and put it back in a different order.
   228	
   229	"It's a sitting," said Lira from the window seat. "They'll want him to say *They are mine* and sign. Nobody straps for that."
   230	
   231	"I strap for the road," said Brom.
   232	
   233	She did not laugh at him. That was how Cael knew she had been thinking about the road too.
   234	
   235	Karis came last, with the attestation wallet under her arm and her coat already over the back of the chair as if she meant to sleep in it. She checked the wallet's contents leaf against its contents, which she had done at supper and would do again in the coach, and then she sat on the end of the bed beside Brom's kit and looked at the sill.
   236	
   237	There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
   238	
   239	Cael told them.
   240	
   241	He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
   242	
   243	Karis sat very still with the wallet on her knees.
   244	
   245	"My room," she said.
   246	
   247	"If you'll have it."
   248	
   249	"He asked for my room." She said it slowly, as if she were reading it off a page in a hand she did not know. "Out of every room on the hill." Then she looked up, and Cael saw that she was not frightened, or not only. "Yes. Tell him yes. Tell him I'll have the girl sent home early that day, and the kettle on, and every paper on the table turned over." She stopped. "No. Don't tell him the last part. I'll just do it."
   250	
   251	"I'll bring the kettle," said Brom.
   252	
   253	Lira did not say anything for a while. She was looking out at the rain. When she spoke it was to the glass.
   254	
   255	"Two years he's sat at the middle of the bench," she said. "Two years, and never once put anything of his on the table. Not a cup. Not a word he didn't have to." She turned round. "Good. That's all. Good."
   256	
   257	Brom stood and shouldered his kit bag. At the door he stopped, and put his hand flat to the left side of his coat, where the letter was, the one he had carried sealed since the forty-fifth day, and kept it there a moment, as if he were checking that the road had not already taken something.
   258	
   259	"The hour before light," he said, and went.
   260	
   261	Cael wrote it that night with the rain at last beginning to slacken against the glass.
   262	
   263	*The bridge chained at the third ring. The ferry tied up. One road to the seat tomorrow, round by the upper ferry, four hours. Seln came up the stair at dusk with a case under his coat and set it on my sill. He's going to open it. He isn't sure. I believe him more because he isn't.*

