     1	# Chapter 31 — Four Exchanges
     2	
     3	The count was slow, and the four men moved on it.
     4	
     5	Cael had seen drills like it on the second floor, when Rooke wanted a team to learn to arrive. You did not run at your mark. You walked, and the count told you how long you had, and you timed your stride so that you came onto your mark on the last word, at the same moment as everyone else came onto theirs. Four men arriving together round a coach were not four men. They were a ring. A ring could be drawn tight by one more word.
     6	
     7	They came in from the two ditches and the two ends, one at each corner of the coach, unhurried, with their staves still at their belts and their hands open. *One.* Their feet touched the chalk at the same instant. *Two.* The lead's voice went along the cut like a man calling a dance.
     8	
     9	Lira went on two and a half.
    10	
    11	He saw her go out of the corner of his eye, because he was already moving the other way. She did not go at any of them. She went to the place behind the coach where the nearest man's next stride was going to come down, and she was standing in it, feet set, when his foot arrived. He came onto her instead of onto his mark. It was not a blow. It was worse than a blow, for a man in a drill: it was the wrong ground, a beat early, and he stumbled into her and off her and lost the count.
    12	
    13	*Three.*
    14	
    15	Her second burst took her across the coach's tail and up the north camber to the ditch side, low, so fast that the dust she left behind her hung in the air in the shape of the road she had taken. The man coming up out of the ditch found her where his third stride should have been and stopped dead with his hands up.
    16	
    17	The lead's voice did not falter. He said *four*. But there were only two men on their marks for it, and a ring with two men in it is a pair of men.
    18	
    19	That was all Cael saw. He did not see the third burst or the fourth. He had his own road to take, and it went straight up the crown at the woman in the hood.
    20	
    21	---
    22	
    23	She had not moved since she came out of the hollow.
    24	
    25	She stood on the flat patch of crown halfway between the coach's horses and the cart, three-quarters turned, with her hands loose at her sides and her weight even. She was not looking at him. She was looking at a place on the road about a pace in front of him and a little to his left. He did not understand that until later.
    26	
    27	He went at her because she was the key. Every one of the fourteen was hers. He had felt them through his palm and he had felt whose they were, the way you can tell, without being told, that all the letters in a bundle are in one hand. If she was down, or moving, or frightened, she could not lay a fifteenth. He did not mean to hurt her. He meant to make her move.
    28	
    29	He spent the Wind at ten paces.
    30	
    31	It was a good burst. He had been holding the band down so long that the full thing came out of him almost with relief: the gather low in the belly, the hip loading like a drawn bow, and the shove at his back. The road came up under him. Six feet out from the place he had left, it stopped.
    32	
    33	Not the road. Him.
    34	
    35	He hit something in the air in front of his chest. It was not a wall, because a wall would have had an edge, and a surface, and a feel. It was a single point, about the height of his breastbone, and it did not give at all. The burst went into it and stopped there, all of it, the way a cart stops when its wheel finds a post in the dark. He went back off it onto his heels. His breath went out of him. The landing beat locked his legs where they stood, the same short pause it had always held him in, and for that pause he could do nothing but stand on the chalk with the point six inches in front of him and know it was there.
    36	
    37	She had laid it while he was in the air.
    38	
    39	He saw now where she had been looking. She had been looking at the place he was going to be.
    40	
    41	He went left. He went round the point, low, with his weight forward and his left foot reaching for the crown. And his foot came down a hand's breadth short of where he had sent it, on nothing, on the air above the chalk, and stayed there. The point under it held his boot like a step. His knee took the jolt. He was standing on one leg on the road and on the other leg on air, and the air was harder than the road.
    42	
    43	She had not lifted a hand. She had not moved her feet. She had not even turned toward him.
    44	
    45	He came off the point and went back. He had to think.
    46	
    47	*She doesn't hit*, he thought. *She takes.*
    48	
    49	That was the first thing. Every fighter he had ever faced had come at him with something, a burst or a course or a lane or a fist. They had put a thing into the space between them and asked him to deal with it. She did the opposite. She took the space away. Every step he meant to take, she got there first and took the place he meant to put his foot, and left him with one less step in the world than he had had a moment before.
    50	
    51	She was reading him. That was the second thing, and it made him cold all down his back. In the outer court he had watched her get onto a horse without the half beat, the little gathering of the body that tells you a person has decided to move. Somebody had trained it out of her. And because she had none, she could see it in everybody else. She saw his half beat. She saw where his weight was going before his weight knew it. She laid her point there and waited for him to arrive.
    52	
    53	Somewhere at the back of him, in its sealed place, the quiet thing from the stair stirred, the way it always stirred when he was cornered, and offered to take him out of every eye in the cut.
    54	
    55	He did not look at it. It stayed where it was.
    56	
    57	Behind him something hit something with a sound like a sack of grain dropped off a cart.
    58	
    59	He turned his head for the length of one breath. Two of the four had given up on the ring. They had seen where their work was, and it was not at the coach. It was in the channel along the south bank, under the lip, where the frame left its one way open to the culvert, and where Brom was standing in it.
    60	
    61	The first of them had gone at him square. He was a heavy man, nearly Brom's height, and he had come down the channel at a run with his shoulder low. Brom had not stepped into him or away from him. He had simply been there, where the man was going, with his feet set and his arms loose and his chin in. The man had arrived. The sound had been the man arriving.
    62	
    63	He was on the chalk now, two yards back up the channel, on one knee. Brom had not moved a foot.
