    65	That was one.
    66	
    67	Cael turned back to her.
    68	
    69	He had backed off two steps to think, and the two steps were gone.
    70	
    71	He found that out when he went to take a third, and his heel came down against air. There was a point behind him now, at the height of his calf, where there had been nothing when he walked through that place. She had laid it while he was looking at Brom. He had not even felt her do it.
    72	
    73	So that was the third thing. She did not take anything back.
    74	
    75	Every point she had laid since he came at her was still there. The one at his breastbone. The one under his left foot. The one behind his calf. He could not see any of them. He could feel each one if he moved toward it, the way you feel furniture in a dark room by walking into it. And she was not lifting them to lay new ones. She was laying new ones as well. The space round him was the space round him minus three, and in a moment it would be minus four.
    76	
    77	She did not need to hurry. She had all morning. She had laid fourteen before he ever came into the cut.
    78	
    79	He tried the push.
    80	
    81	He put his right palm flat against the point at his breastbone, where his chest had found it, and leaned, and then did not lean. He gave it the push at fighting load, the thing he had taken from the Bronze at Ardenmere, the thing he had given a Gold five times in one sitting at Norhold.
    82	
    83	The point did not move.
    84	
    85	He did. He went backward off his own push as if he had shoved at the side of a house. It threw him two yards along the crown to the north, faster than he could have gone on his feet, and that was useful. For a moment he was out of the pocket she had been building round him. He took it. He used the second push the same way, against the same point from a new side, and it threw him again, west, toward the coach. The third threw him back east. The point stayed where it was every time. It was the most solid thing on the road. He was using it the way a swimmer uses the side of a bath.
    86	
    87	The fourth push came, and the right shoulder spoke, low, the way it always spoke at the fourth.
    88	
    89	He stopped.
    90	
    91	Four pushes and she had not taken a step. Four pushes and he was still in the cut, with three points round him and the woman on her flat patch of crown, and every push had moved nobody but him.
    92	
    93	He tried to make her move them.
    94	
    95	He went wide, fast, toward the north ditch, as if he meant to run along it past her to the cart. She laid a point in front of him at the ditch's edge. It was further from her than any she had laid yet, and he saw what it cost her. She was still for an instant. Not the stillness of a woman waiting. The stillness of a woman doing something, the way a person goes still to thread a needle. Half a second. Less. Then she was waiting again.
    96	
    97	He turned before he reached the point, and came back, and went wide the other way, toward the south. She laid one there. Half a second.
    98	
    99	*Range costs her*, he thought. *Not much. Something.*
   100	
   101	He went wide a third time, north again, and she did not lay anything at all. She simply watched him go, and when he turned to come back, the place he was turning into had a point in it, close to her, cheap.
   102	
   103	She had learned him faster than he had learned her. She had stopped biting.
   104	
   105	---
   106	
   107	He had a moment, then, because she let him have it.
   108	
   109	He looked round the cut for the length of a breath. Lira was on the ground.
   110	
   111	She was at the edge of the north ditch, where the camber fell away into it, sitting in the chalk with one leg out in front of her and her hands flat behind her. She had spent four bursts. He had not seen the third or the fourth, and he did not know which had put her there. He knew only that her right foot was turned at an angle no foot was meant to hold, and that she was holding very still. One of the four stood over her, a yard off, with his stave across his body in both hands. He did not touch her. He did not need to. He stood between her and the coach and looked at her, and she looked back at him.
   112	
   113	Custody. Not assault. They did not want her. They wanted her out of the way.
   114	
   115	At the near horse, Karis had her right hand flat in the air a hand's breadth above the animal's foreleg, as if she were warming it at a fire.
   116	
   117	He knew what she was doing as soon as he saw it, because he had heard her explain the difference on the night of the gate, counting the words off on her fingers. Ternhall's anchors were her own, three footholds that drank the heat of a contact down into a floor. The Lattice Path was lines strung across the air. These were neither. These were points, and they held. But something ran between them. When she had put her hand out to the horse's leg she had found it, the way you find a rope across a dark yard: a line, taut, between the point fixed against the horse's foreleg and one of the fourteen in the bank. And she had put her heat into it.
   118	
   119	It frayed.
   120	
   121	He could not see it. He saw the horse's leg shift, a finger's breadth, as if somebody had cut one strand of a halter. He saw Karis's face, set and white, and her right hand, and the skin of her fingers going red from the tips down. She moved the hand an inch along the line she could not see and put her heat into it again.
   122	
   123	The coachman sat on his box with his reins in his hands and could not let go of them. He was talking to his horses in the same low steady voice he always used. His hands were held where they were, in the air in front of him, as if he were driving a coach that was not moving.
   124	
   125	Brom was still in the channel.
   126	
   127	The second man came at him while Cael was watching. He was lighter than the first, and quicker, and he had learned from the first. He did not come at Brom square. He came at the left side, low, and at the last stride turned and drove his shoulder up into Brom's left shoulder from underneath, with his whole weight behind it, the way a man drives a post into a hole.
   128	
